1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,704
It's seven o'clock on a February morning

2
00:00:02,704 --> 00:00:04,191
in nineteen sixty-two.

3
00:00:04,371 --> 00:00:06,676
The kitchen is the warmest room in

4
00:00:06,676 --> 00:00:07,354
the house.

5
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:10,367
The percolator is bubbling on the counter,

6
00:00:10,367 --> 00:00:12,930
the radio on the windowsill is talking

7
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:15,358
about an astronaut named John Glenn,

8
00:00:15,358 --> 00:00:17,651
and your mother is standing at the

9
00:00:17,651 --> 00:00:18,933
stove in her apron,

10
00:00:18,933 --> 00:00:20,956
with a spatula in one hand and

11
00:00:20,956 --> 00:00:23,452
a cigarette in an ashtray beside her.

12
00:00:23,801 --> 00:00:25,387
If you grew up in America in

13
00:00:25,387 --> 00:00:27,652
those years, you could walk through that

14
00:00:27,652 --> 00:00:29,351
kitchen with your eyes closed.

15
00:00:29,531 --> 00:00:31,406
You'd know which drawer stuck,

16
00:00:31,406 --> 00:00:33,717
which cupboard held the good glasses,

17
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:35,967
and exactly where the cookie jar was

18
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:36,404
hidden.

19
00:00:36,584 --> 00:00:38,416
But some of the things in that

20
00:00:38,416 --> 00:00:41,164
room have disappeared so completely that most

21
00:00:41,164 --> 00:00:43,607
people under fifty have never even heard

22
00:00:43,607 --> 00:00:44,096
of them.

23
00:00:44,445 --> 00:00:47,004
So tonight, let's take a slow walk

24
00:00:47,004 --> 00:00:50,843
through the American kitchen of nineteen sixty-two.

25
00:00:51,023 --> 00:00:52,300
Cupboard by cupboard.

26
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:53,792
Drawer by drawer.

27
00:00:53,972 --> 00:00:55,721
And let's see how many of these

28
00:00:55,721 --> 00:00:57,188
things you still remember.

29
00:00:57,368 --> 00:00:58,820
I have a feeling it's going to

30
00:00:58,820 --> 00:00:59,933
be more than you think.

31
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:02,047
On the radio that morning,

32
00:01:02,047 --> 00:01:04,150
the news was all about one man.

33
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:06,025
On February twentieth,

34
00:01:06,025 --> 00:01:09,646
nineteen sixty-two, John Glenn became the first

35
00:01:09,646 --> 00:01:11,804
American to orbit the Earth,

36
00:01:11,804 --> 00:01:14,578
circling it three times in a capsule

37
00:01:14,578 --> 00:01:16,427
called Friendship Seven.

38
00:01:16,607 --> 00:01:17,796
Across the country,

39
00:01:17,796 --> 00:01:20,174
mothers stood at their stoves with the

40
00:01:20,174 --> 00:01:21,176
radio turned up,

41
00:01:21,176 --> 00:01:23,554
and children were allowed to watch the

42
00:01:23,554 --> 00:01:25,744
launch on the classroom television.

43
00:01:26,095 --> 00:01:28,053
Let's start with the color of the

44
00:01:28,053 --> 00:01:28,765
room itself.

45
00:01:28,945 --> 00:01:31,562
Today, most kitchens are white,

46
00:01:31,562 --> 00:01:33,925
or gray, or stainless steel.

47
00:01:34,105 --> 00:01:35,861
But in nineteen sixty-two,

48
00:01:35,861 --> 00:01:38,900
the American kitchen was bursting with color.

49
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:39,940
Turquoise.

50
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:40,456
Pink.

51
00:01:40,636 --> 00:01:41,530
Sunny yellow.

52
00:01:41,710 --> 00:01:42,999
Mint green.

53
00:01:43,179 --> 00:01:43,994
And a warm,

54
00:01:43,994 --> 00:01:48,067
coppery brown that the manufacturers called coppertone.

55
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:50,797
The color wasn't just on the walls.

56
00:01:50,977 --> 00:01:52,879
It was on the refrigerator,

57
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:54,711
the stove, the countertop,

58
00:01:54,711 --> 00:01:57,037
and sometimes even the telephone.

59
00:01:57,217 --> 00:02:00,257
Appliance makers had learned that a housewife

60
00:02:00,257 --> 00:02:02,960
who bought a pink stove would eventually

61
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:05,460
want a pink refrigerator to match it.

62
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:07,751
Color was a way of selling the

63
00:02:07,751 --> 00:02:10,214
whole kitchen, one piece at a time.

64
00:02:10,564 --> 00:02:12,180
Here's the twist that a lot of

65
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:13,095
people get wrong.

66
00:02:13,275 --> 00:02:15,865
The famous avocado green and harvest gold

67
00:02:15,865 --> 00:02:18,265
that people picture when they think of

68
00:02:18,265 --> 00:02:20,917
a retro kitchen didn't really arrive until

69
00:02:20,917 --> 00:02:22,180
later in the decade.

70
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,087
In nineteen sixty-two,

71
00:02:24,087 --> 00:02:27,069
the palette was still the soft pastels

72
00:02:27,069 --> 00:02:28,247
of the fifties.

73
00:02:28,427 --> 00:02:29,866
Avocado was coming.

74
00:02:30,046 --> 00:02:31,312
But it wasn't there yet.

75
00:02:31,661 --> 00:02:33,641
On the floor was linoleum,

76
00:02:33,641 --> 00:02:34,707
or vinyl tile,

77
00:02:34,707 --> 00:02:37,525
often laid in a checkerboard pattern.

78
00:02:37,705 --> 00:02:39,094
On the counters,

79
00:02:39,094 --> 00:02:42,655
Formica, sometimes in a pattern of little

80
00:02:42,655 --> 00:02:44,392
boomerangs or stars.

81
00:02:44,572 --> 00:02:46,697
It was all designed for one thing.

82
00:02:46,877 --> 00:02:48,998
To wipe clean in a single pass,

83
00:02:48,998 --> 00:02:51,324
because the woman of the house was

84
00:02:51,324 --> 00:02:53,308
expected to keep it spotless.

85
00:02:53,659 --> 00:02:56,316
At the window hung café curtains,

86
00:02:56,316 --> 00:02:58,732
covering just the bottom half,

87
00:02:58,732 --> 00:03:01,229
in gingham checks or a print of

88
00:03:01,229 --> 00:03:05,176
teapots, roosters, or little bunches of cherries.

89
00:03:05,356 --> 00:03:07,520
Your mother might have sewn them herself

90
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:08,711
on her sewing machine.

91
00:03:08,891 --> 00:03:10,706
They matched the dish towels,

92
00:03:10,706 --> 00:03:12,521
which matched the potholders,

93
00:03:12,521 --> 00:03:14,899
which very often had been crocheted by

94
00:03:14,899 --> 00:03:15,776
a grandmother.

95
00:03:16,125 --> 00:03:17,910
In the middle of the room stood

96
00:03:17,910 --> 00:03:18,831
the dinette set.

97
00:03:19,011 --> 00:03:21,602
A table with a Formica top and

98
00:03:21,602 --> 00:03:23,330
a shiny chrome edge,

99
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:26,526
and four or six chairs upholstered in

100
00:03:26,526 --> 00:03:27,045
vinyl.

101
00:03:27,225 --> 00:03:28,309
Red, or yellow,

102
00:03:28,309 --> 00:03:30,622
or gray with a pattern of little

103
00:03:30,622 --> 00:03:31,706
sparkles in it.

104
00:03:31,886 --> 00:03:34,455
In summer, those chairs stuck to the

105
00:03:34,455 --> 00:03:35,810
backs of your legs.

106
00:03:35,990 --> 00:03:37,778
In winter, they were cold.

107
00:03:38,128 --> 00:03:39,281
Under that table,

108
00:03:39,281 --> 00:03:41,723
generations of children built forts,

109
00:03:41,723 --> 00:03:43,351
hid from their brothers,

110
00:03:43,351 --> 00:03:46,267
and tied their sisters' shoelaces together.

111
00:03:46,447 --> 00:03:48,764
The underside was where the chewing gum

112
00:03:48,764 --> 00:03:50,487
went when nobody was looking.

113
00:03:50,667 --> 00:03:52,462
And if you ever got down there

114
00:03:52,462 --> 00:03:54,495
again today, I'd bet the gum would

115
00:03:54,495 --> 00:03:55,393
still be there.

116
00:03:55,742 --> 00:03:57,856
That table was where everything happened.

117
00:03:58,036 --> 00:03:59,731
Breakfast before school.

118
00:03:59,911 --> 00:04:01,640
Homework in the afternoon.

119
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:03,957
Your father reading the evening paper.

120
00:04:04,137 --> 00:04:06,161
Your mother paying the bills,

121
00:04:06,161 --> 00:04:07,766
writing a grocery list,

122
00:04:07,766 --> 00:04:09,999
or talking on the phone with her

123
00:04:09,999 --> 00:04:10,487
sister.

124
00:04:10,667 --> 00:04:11,804
And late at night,

125
00:04:11,804 --> 00:04:13,952
the grown-ups sitting with coffee,

126
00:04:13,952 --> 00:04:17,047
talking quietly about things the children weren't

127
00:04:17,047 --> 00:04:18,121
supposed to hear.

128
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:20,654
And in the middle of the table,

129
00:04:20,654 --> 00:04:22,766
almost always, sat an ashtray.

130
00:04:22,946 --> 00:04:24,974
Glass, or heavy ceramic,

131
00:04:24,974 --> 00:04:28,101
sometimes a souvenir from a vacation.

132
00:04:28,281 --> 00:04:29,848
In nineteen sixty-two,

133
00:04:29,848 --> 00:04:32,697
a large share of American adults smoked,

134
00:04:32,697 --> 00:04:35,262
and smoking at the kitchen table was

135
00:04:35,262 --> 00:04:37,755
as normal as drinking coffee there.

136
00:04:37,935 --> 00:04:38,997
Two years later,

137
00:04:38,997 --> 00:04:41,852
the Surgeon General's report would begin to

138
00:04:41,852 --> 00:04:42,648
change that.

139
00:04:42,828 --> 00:04:43,653
But not yet.

140
00:04:44,002 --> 00:04:45,207
Next to the table,

141
00:04:45,207 --> 00:04:47,616
there was often a little step stool,

142
00:04:47,616 --> 00:04:49,957
the kind with steps that folded out

143
00:04:49,957 --> 00:04:51,630
from underneath the seat.

144
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:52,876
It was a chair,

145
00:04:52,876 --> 00:04:54,794
a ladder, and a place for a

146
00:04:54,794 --> 00:04:57,210
small child to sit and watch their

147
00:04:57,210 --> 00:04:58,916
mother cook, all in one.

148
00:04:59,096 --> 00:05:02,085
Somebody in your family probably still has

149
00:05:02,085 --> 00:05:02,370
one.

150
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:03,854
Now turn around,

151
00:05:03,854 --> 00:05:05,413
and look at the stove.

152
00:05:05,593 --> 00:05:07,207
It might have been gas,

153
00:05:07,207 --> 00:05:09,664
with a little blue pilot light that

154
00:05:09,664 --> 00:05:10,717
never went out,

155
00:05:10,717 --> 00:05:12,823
and a box of wooden matches on

156
00:05:12,823 --> 00:05:14,788
a shelf nearby just in case.

157
00:05:14,968 --> 00:05:16,870
Or it might have been electric,

158
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:19,264
with coiled burners that glowed orange.

159
00:05:19,444 --> 00:05:20,999
Either way, it was big,

160
00:05:20,999 --> 00:05:22,756
white or colored to match,

161
00:05:22,756 --> 00:05:24,851
and it was the busiest thing in

162
00:05:24,851 --> 00:05:25,527
the house.

163
00:05:25,878 --> 00:05:27,774
On the back of the stove sat

164
00:05:27,774 --> 00:05:29,670
the salt and pepper shakers,

165
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:31,973
often shaped like something silly.

166
00:05:32,153 --> 00:05:33,801
A pair of chickens.

167
00:05:33,981 --> 00:05:35,665
Two little Dutch children.

168
00:05:35,845 --> 00:05:37,319
A cowboy boot.

169
00:05:37,499 --> 00:05:38,729
And next to them,

170
00:05:38,729 --> 00:05:40,464
a can or a jar for bacon

171
00:05:40,464 --> 00:05:40,971
grease.

172
00:05:41,151 --> 00:05:43,770
Every drop was poured off and saved,

173
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:45,879
to fry eggs the next morning,

174
00:05:45,879 --> 00:05:47,989
or to season the green beans.

175
00:05:48,339 --> 00:05:50,514
Some of the fancier stoves had a

176
00:05:50,514 --> 00:05:53,505
built-in griddle in the middle for pancakes,

177
00:05:53,505 --> 00:05:55,340
a clock with a timer on the

178
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:57,719
back panel, and even a second oven.

179
00:05:57,899 --> 00:06:00,279
Your mother knew every quirk of hers.

180
00:06:00,459 --> 00:06:01,876
Which burner ran hot.

181
00:06:02,056 --> 00:06:04,144
Which corner of the oven burned the

182
00:06:04,144 --> 00:06:04,622
cookies.

183
00:06:04,802 --> 00:06:07,198
She adjusted for all of it without

184
00:06:07,198 --> 00:06:08,749
ever opening a manual.

185
00:06:09,099 --> 00:06:11,688
Now let's open the refrigerator.

186
00:06:11,868 --> 00:06:13,604
In nineteen sixty-two,

187
00:06:13,604 --> 00:06:16,366
it was probably a single tall door,

188
00:06:16,366 --> 00:06:19,129
with rounded edges and a big chrome

189
00:06:19,129 --> 00:06:19,681
handle.

190
00:06:19,861 --> 00:06:21,044
And at the top,

191
00:06:21,044 --> 00:06:24,592
inside, there was a tiny freezer compartment,

192
00:06:24,592 --> 00:06:27,273
barely big enough for two ice cube

193
00:06:27,273 --> 00:06:29,718
trays and a box of frozen peas.

194
00:06:30,067 --> 00:06:32,448
And that freezer compartment had a problem.

195
00:06:32,628 --> 00:06:33,405
Frost.

196
00:06:33,585 --> 00:06:34,640
In many homes,

197
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:37,805
the frost slowly grew thicker and thicker,

198
00:06:37,805 --> 00:06:40,667
until the freezer door wouldn't close.

199
00:06:40,847 --> 00:06:42,435
So every few weeks,

200
00:06:42,435 --> 00:06:44,690
somebody had to defrost it.

201
00:06:44,870 --> 00:06:47,122
You unplugged the refrigerator,

202
00:06:47,122 --> 00:06:48,211
emptied it out,

203
00:06:48,211 --> 00:06:50,535
and put pans of hot water inside

204
00:06:50,535 --> 00:06:51,697
to melt the ice.

205
00:06:51,877 --> 00:06:54,820
Sometimes your father got impatient and chipped

206
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:56,010
at it with a knife.

207
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:59,853
Frost-free refrigerators were already on the market,

208
00:06:59,853 --> 00:07:01,867
and they were one of the great

209
00:07:01,867 --> 00:07:03,210
luxuries of the age.

210
00:07:03,390 --> 00:07:04,821
But they cost more,

211
00:07:04,821 --> 00:07:07,457
and plenty of families kept the old

212
00:07:07,457 --> 00:07:09,114
one running for years.

213
00:07:09,294 --> 00:07:11,047
If you ever chipped ice out of

214
00:07:11,047 --> 00:07:12,801
a freezer with a butter knife,

215
00:07:12,801 --> 00:07:14,495
you know exactly what I mean.

216
00:07:14,845 --> 00:07:16,959
Then there were the ice cube trays.

217
00:07:17,139 --> 00:07:18,356
Made of aluminum,

218
00:07:18,356 --> 00:07:20,503
with a metal grid inside and a

219
00:07:20,503 --> 00:07:21,434
lever on top.

220
00:07:21,614 --> 00:07:23,042
You pulled the lever,

221
00:07:23,042 --> 00:07:25,422
and the grid cracked the ice loose,

222
00:07:25,422 --> 00:07:25,898
mostly.

223
00:07:26,078 --> 00:07:28,156
Half the cubes broke into pieces.

224
00:07:28,336 --> 00:07:30,073
And if your fingers were wet,

225
00:07:30,073 --> 00:07:32,108
they'd stick to the cold metal for

226
00:07:32,108 --> 00:07:33,306
a terrifying second.

227
00:07:33,656 --> 00:07:35,356
Inside the refrigerator,

228
00:07:35,356 --> 00:07:37,766
you'd find a glass bottle of milk,

229
00:07:37,766 --> 00:07:39,396
delivered that morning,

230
00:07:39,396 --> 00:07:41,451
and a stick of real butter in

231
00:07:41,451 --> 00:07:42,514
a covered dish.

232
00:07:42,694 --> 00:07:44,867
A jar of bacon grease sat on

233
00:07:44,867 --> 00:07:47,119
the stove, not in the fridge,

234
00:07:47,119 --> 00:07:49,370
because nobody wasted grease.

235
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:51,767
And there was always a bowl of

236
00:07:51,767 --> 00:07:54,059
leftovers covered with a plate,

237
00:07:54,059 --> 00:07:56,425
or with waxed paper and a rubber

238
00:07:56,425 --> 00:07:56,794
band.

239
00:07:57,144 --> 00:07:59,365
And here's something you won't find on

240
00:07:59,365 --> 00:08:00,709
that refrigerator door.

241
00:08:00,889 --> 00:08:01,887
Magnets.

242
00:08:02,067 --> 00:08:05,051
The little decorative fridge magnets that cover

243
00:08:05,051 --> 00:08:07,908
kitchens today didn't become common until the

244
00:08:07,908 --> 00:08:09,114
nineteen seventies.

245
00:08:09,294 --> 00:08:10,752
In nineteen sixty-two,

246
00:08:10,752 --> 00:08:13,668
the children's drawings and the school lunch

247
00:08:13,668 --> 00:08:15,523
menu were held up with tape,

248
00:08:15,523 --> 00:08:17,445
or tucked into the frame of a

249
00:08:17,445 --> 00:08:17,909
mirror.

250
00:08:18,259 --> 00:08:20,592
On the kitchen wall hung the telephone.

251
00:08:20,772 --> 00:08:22,120
Black, or white,

252
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:24,310
or in a color to match the

253
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:24,731
room.

254
00:08:24,911 --> 00:08:26,425
It had a rotary dial,

255
00:08:26,425 --> 00:08:27,722
and it was rented,

256
00:08:27,722 --> 00:08:30,461
not owned, from the telephone company.

257
00:08:30,641 --> 00:08:32,418
Most families had only one,

258
00:08:32,418 --> 00:08:34,853
and the kitchen was the natural place

259
00:08:34,853 --> 00:08:37,552
for it, because that's where Mother spent

260
00:08:37,552 --> 00:08:38,605
most of her day.

261
00:08:38,956 --> 00:08:40,105
And it had a cord.

262
00:08:40,285 --> 00:08:41,587
A long, curly cord,

263
00:08:41,587 --> 00:08:44,328
that over the years stretched longer and

264
00:08:44,328 --> 00:08:46,932
longer, as your mother walked from the

265
00:08:46,932 --> 00:08:48,988
stove to the sink to the table

266
00:08:48,988 --> 00:08:50,153
while she talked.

267
00:08:50,333 --> 00:08:52,814
Teenagers stretched it all the way into

268
00:08:52,814 --> 00:08:54,659
the pantry and shut the door,

269
00:08:54,659 --> 00:08:56,440
hoping for a little privacy.

270
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:59,279
The cord always ended up hopelessly tangled.

271
00:08:59,629 --> 00:09:01,110
The newest telephone,

272
00:09:01,110 --> 00:09:03,719
the one every teenage girl wanted for

273
00:09:03,719 --> 00:09:06,258
her bedroom, was the Princess phone.

274
00:09:06,438 --> 00:09:09,387
Bell introduced it in nineteen fifty-nine.

275
00:09:09,567 --> 00:09:11,116
It was small and slim,

276
00:09:11,116 --> 00:09:12,524
came in soft colors,

277
00:09:12,524 --> 00:09:14,355
and had a dial that lit up

278
00:09:14,355 --> 00:09:14,989
at night.

279
00:09:15,169 --> 00:09:17,077
The advertisements said,

280
00:09:17,077 --> 00:09:19,064
it's little, it's lovely,

281
00:09:19,064 --> 00:09:19,859
it lights.

282
00:09:20,209 --> 00:09:21,960
Next to the kitchen phone,

283
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:24,182
there was usually a little pad of

284
00:09:24,182 --> 00:09:26,068
paper, a pencil on a string,

285
00:09:26,068 --> 00:09:28,561
and a list of important numbers taped

286
00:09:28,561 --> 00:09:29,369
to the wall.

287
00:09:29,549 --> 00:09:30,431
The doctor.

288
00:09:30,611 --> 00:09:31,586
The pharmacy.

289
00:09:31,766 --> 00:09:32,440
Grandma.

290
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:35,808
And telephone numbers in those days often

291
00:09:35,808 --> 00:09:37,285
began with letters,

292
00:09:37,285 --> 00:09:40,473
an exchange name like Pennsylvania six or

293
00:09:40,473 --> 00:09:41,873
Butterfield eight.

294
00:09:42,053 --> 00:09:44,122
Many of you can still recite your

295
00:09:44,122 --> 00:09:45,188
childhood number.

296
00:09:45,538 --> 00:09:48,179
Long distance calls were expensive,

297
00:09:48,179 --> 00:09:50,669
so they were saved for birthdays,

298
00:09:50,669 --> 00:09:52,329
holidays and bad news.

299
00:09:52,509 --> 00:09:54,503
When the phone rang late at night,

300
00:09:54,503 --> 00:09:55,969
everybody's heart jumped.

301
00:09:56,149 --> 00:09:58,934
And when Grandma called from another state,

302
00:09:58,934 --> 00:10:01,007
the whole family lined up to say

303
00:10:01,007 --> 00:10:01,914
a quick hello,

304
00:10:01,914 --> 00:10:04,440
while somebody watched the clock on the

305
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:04,764
wall.

306
00:10:05,114 --> 00:10:07,227
Now let's look along the counter.

307
00:10:07,407 --> 00:10:08,904
First, the coffee.

308
00:10:09,084 --> 00:10:10,848
In nineteen sixty-two,

309
00:10:10,848 --> 00:10:13,333
nobody had a drip coffee maker.

310
00:10:13,513 --> 00:10:14,802
The famous Mr.

311
00:10:14,982 --> 00:10:18,825
Coffee machine wouldn't arrive until nineteen seventy-two.

312
00:10:19,005 --> 00:10:21,446
Instead, there was the percolator,

313
00:10:21,446 --> 00:10:23,599
a tall metal pot with a little

314
00:10:23,599 --> 00:10:24,891
glass knob on top.

315
00:10:25,241 --> 00:10:26,707
As the coffee heated,

316
00:10:26,707 --> 00:10:29,149
it bubbled up into that glass knob,

317
00:10:29,149 --> 00:10:30,265
again and again,

318
00:10:30,265 --> 00:10:32,498
with a sound like a little drum.

319
00:10:32,678 --> 00:10:33,293
Blub.

320
00:10:33,473 --> 00:10:33,879
Blub.

321
00:10:34,059 --> 00:10:34,501
Blub.

322
00:10:34,681 --> 00:10:36,521
You could tell how strong it was

323
00:10:36,521 --> 00:10:38,477
getting by the color in the glass.

324
00:10:38,657 --> 00:10:40,511
That sound, and that smell,

325
00:10:40,511 --> 00:10:43,533
meant morning in millions of American homes.

326
00:10:43,883 --> 00:10:45,950
Next to it stood the canister set.

327
00:10:46,130 --> 00:10:48,299
Four matching containers,

328
00:10:48,299 --> 00:10:50,467
from biggest to smallest,

329
00:10:50,467 --> 00:10:52,289
labeled flour, sugar,

330
00:10:52,289 --> 00:10:53,677
coffee, and tea.

331
00:10:53,857 --> 00:10:55,152
Some were metal,

332
00:10:55,152 --> 00:10:56,852
painted with flowers.

333
00:10:57,032 --> 00:10:58,576
Some were ceramic,

334
00:10:58,576 --> 00:11:00,550
or glass with red lids.

335
00:11:00,730 --> 00:11:01,768
And very often,

336
00:11:01,768 --> 00:11:04,746
the one labeled tea actually held something

337
00:11:04,746 --> 00:11:07,238
else, like rubber bands or the money

338
00:11:07,238 --> 00:11:08,346
for the milkman.

339
00:11:08,696 --> 00:11:10,333
There was a bread box,

340
00:11:10,333 --> 00:11:12,939
made of metal with a roll-top door,

341
00:11:12,939 --> 00:11:15,619
keeping the loaf of white bread soft

342
00:11:15,619 --> 00:11:17,182
for a few extra days.

343
00:11:17,362 --> 00:11:18,747
And a cookie jar,

344
00:11:18,747 --> 00:11:20,294
shaped like a bear,

345
00:11:20,294 --> 00:11:21,109
or an owl,

346
00:11:21,109 --> 00:11:22,738
or a little cottage.

347
00:11:22,918 --> 00:11:24,946
The lid always made a loud clink

348
00:11:24,946 --> 00:11:26,151
when you lifted it,

349
00:11:26,151 --> 00:11:27,989
which is why so many kids got

350
00:11:27,989 --> 00:11:28,433
caught.

351
00:11:28,782 --> 00:11:30,140
And in pride of place,

352
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:31,929
there was the electric mixer.

353
00:11:32,109 --> 00:11:34,556
Very often, a Sunbeam Mixmaster,

354
00:11:34,556 --> 00:11:37,234
with two glass bowls that turned on

355
00:11:37,234 --> 00:11:38,610
a little platform,

356
00:11:38,610 --> 00:11:41,211
and two beaters that whirred away.

357
00:11:41,391 --> 00:11:43,212
For decades, it was one of the

358
00:11:43,212 --> 00:11:45,640
most common wedding presents in America.

359
00:11:45,820 --> 00:11:47,592
And when your mother was done,

360
00:11:47,592 --> 00:11:49,245
you got to lick the beaters.

361
00:11:49,596 --> 00:11:52,163
The newest gadget on many counters was

362
00:11:52,163 --> 00:11:53,717
an electric can opener.

363
00:11:53,897 --> 00:11:56,190
They had begun appearing in the late

364
00:11:56,190 --> 00:11:58,928
nineteen fifties, and by the early sixties,

365
00:11:58,928 --> 00:12:00,584
they were a status symbol.

366
00:12:00,764 --> 00:12:02,396
You set the can in place,

367
00:12:02,396 --> 00:12:03,571
pressed the lever,

368
00:12:03,571 --> 00:12:05,660
and the can turned all by itself

369
00:12:05,660 --> 00:12:07,684
with a satisfying grinding hum.

370
00:12:07,864 --> 00:12:09,989
People demonstrated them to visitors.

371
00:12:10,339 --> 00:12:11,859
And then there was the toaster.

372
00:12:12,039 --> 00:12:13,942
Chrome, rounded and heavy,

373
00:12:13,942 --> 00:12:16,283
so shiny you could see your face

374
00:12:16,283 --> 00:12:19,283
stretched across the side like a funhouse

375
00:12:19,283 --> 00:12:19,795
mirror.

376
00:12:19,975 --> 00:12:21,761
It had a little lever on the

377
00:12:21,761 --> 00:12:23,611
side, and a dial for light or

378
00:12:23,611 --> 00:12:25,524
dark that nobody ever trusted.

379
00:12:25,704 --> 00:12:28,462
Somebody always ended up scraping burnt toast

380
00:12:28,462 --> 00:12:30,546
over the sink with a butter knife.

381
00:12:30,896 --> 00:12:32,846
Now let's open the drawers.

382
00:12:33,026 --> 00:12:34,271
In the first one,

383
00:12:34,271 --> 00:12:37,345
the silverware, and somewhere in the back,

384
00:12:37,345 --> 00:12:38,297
a church key.

385
00:12:38,477 --> 00:12:41,051
That's what everyone called the flat metal

386
00:12:41,051 --> 00:12:42,645
opener with a pointed end.

387
00:12:42,825 --> 00:12:45,103
Because in nineteen sixty-two,

388
00:12:45,103 --> 00:12:47,456
you couldn't just pull a tab to

389
00:12:47,456 --> 00:12:49,582
open a can of soda or juice.

390
00:12:49,762 --> 00:12:51,882
You had to punch two triangle holes

391
00:12:51,882 --> 00:12:52,548
in the top.

392
00:12:52,899 --> 00:12:54,895
And here's a little piece of history.

393
00:12:55,075 --> 00:12:56,246
That very year,

394
00:12:56,246 --> 00:12:59,601
nineteen sixty-two, a man named Ermal Fraze

395
00:12:59,601 --> 00:13:00,850
in Dayton, Ohio,

396
00:13:00,850 --> 00:13:02,878
patented the pull-tab top,

397
00:13:02,878 --> 00:13:05,453
after he'd been stuck at a picnic

398
00:13:05,453 --> 00:13:07,092
without a church key.

399
00:13:07,272 --> 00:13:09,737
Iron City Beer in Pittsburgh was one

400
00:13:09,737 --> 00:13:11,312
of the first to use it.

401
00:13:11,492 --> 00:13:12,755
Within a few years,

402
00:13:12,755 --> 00:13:15,413
the church key started to disappear from

403
00:13:15,413 --> 00:13:16,542
American drawers.

404
00:13:16,893 --> 00:13:18,680
Then there was the junk drawer.

405
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:20,323
Every kitchen had one.

406
00:13:20,503 --> 00:13:22,009
String, twist ties,

407
00:13:22,009 --> 00:13:24,544
birthday candles, a screwdriver,

408
00:13:24,544 --> 00:13:26,921
a flashlight that didn't work,

409
00:13:26,921 --> 00:13:29,774
keys to locks nobody could identify,

410
00:13:29,774 --> 00:13:32,706
and a stack of trading stamps waiting

411
00:13:32,706 --> 00:13:35,400
to be licked and pasted into their

412
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:35,875
books.

413
00:13:36,225 --> 00:13:37,305
In the cupboards,

414
00:13:37,305 --> 00:13:38,639
you'd find the bowls.

415
00:13:38,819 --> 00:13:40,639
And in nineteen sixty-two,

416
00:13:40,639 --> 00:13:43,159
there's a very good chance they were

417
00:13:43,159 --> 00:13:43,580
Pyrex.

418
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:47,453
Opaque glass mixing bowls in bright colors,

419
00:13:47,453 --> 00:13:49,171
primary red, yellow,

420
00:13:49,171 --> 00:13:50,459
green and blue,

421
00:13:50,459 --> 00:13:53,465
sized to nest one inside the other.

422
00:13:53,645 --> 00:13:56,405
Today, collectors pay real money for a

423
00:13:56,405 --> 00:13:57,059
full set.

424
00:13:57,239 --> 00:13:59,321
Back then, they were simply what you

425
00:13:59,321 --> 00:14:00,362
mixed the cake in.

426
00:14:00,712 --> 00:14:01,699
Next to them,

427
00:14:01,699 --> 00:14:04,814
maybe, a white casserole dish with little

428
00:14:04,814 --> 00:14:06,714
blue flowers on the side.

429
00:14:06,894 --> 00:14:08,364
That was Corning Ware,

430
00:14:08,364 --> 00:14:10,702
introduced in nineteen fifty-eight.

431
00:14:10,882 --> 00:14:13,130
It was made from a material developed

432
00:14:13,130 --> 00:14:15,378
from research into rocket nose cones,

433
00:14:15,378 --> 00:14:17,504
and you could take it straight from

434
00:14:17,504 --> 00:14:18,962
the freezer to the oven.

435
00:14:19,142 --> 00:14:21,977
The blue cornflower pattern became one of

436
00:14:21,977 --> 00:14:25,226
the most familiar designs in American kitchens.

437
00:14:25,577 --> 00:14:27,184
And in the bottom cupboard,

438
00:14:27,184 --> 00:14:28,732
a heavy cast iron skillet,

439
00:14:28,732 --> 00:14:30,815
black and seasoned over many years,

440
00:14:30,815 --> 00:14:33,495
that might have belonged to your grandmother.

441
00:14:33,675 --> 00:14:34,401
Next to it,

442
00:14:34,401 --> 00:14:36,249
an aluminum pressure cooker,

443
00:14:36,249 --> 00:14:38,758
which hissed and rattled on the stove,

444
00:14:38,758 --> 00:14:40,937
and made every child in the house

445
00:14:40,937 --> 00:14:42,521
keep a careful distance.

446
00:14:42,872 --> 00:14:44,705
And in the drawer next to the

447
00:14:44,705 --> 00:14:46,413
stove lived the hand tools.

448
00:14:46,593 --> 00:14:48,240
A hand-cranked egg beater,

449
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:50,585
with two little whisks that spun when

450
00:14:50,585 --> 00:14:51,980
you turned the handle.

451
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:54,923
A flour sifter with a squeaky trigger.

452
00:14:55,103 --> 00:14:57,782
A wooden spoon worn flat on one

453
00:14:57,782 --> 00:14:58,214
side.

454
00:14:58,394 --> 00:14:59,904
A potato masher.

455
00:15:00,084 --> 00:15:01,233
And in some homes,

456
00:15:01,233 --> 00:15:03,659
a metal meat grinder that clamped onto

457
00:15:03,659 --> 00:15:05,064
the edge of the table.

458
00:15:05,415 --> 00:15:07,494
Many of these tools had no motor

459
00:15:07,494 --> 00:15:09,313
at all, and some of them had

460
00:15:09,313 --> 00:15:11,846
been wedding gifts twenty years before.

461
00:15:12,026 --> 00:15:13,016
They were heavy,

462
00:15:13,016 --> 00:15:15,428
simple, and almost impossible to break.

463
00:15:15,608 --> 00:15:16,869
If you inherited one,

464
00:15:16,869 --> 00:15:19,030
there's a fair chance it still works

465
00:15:19,030 --> 00:15:21,912
perfectly today, while the gadgets your children

466
00:15:21,912 --> 00:15:24,133
bought last year have already gone in

467
00:15:24,133 --> 00:15:24,734
the trash.

468
00:15:25,083 --> 00:15:27,370
Now, one of the most important things

469
00:15:27,370 --> 00:15:29,904
in your mother's kitchen didn't come from

470
00:15:29,904 --> 00:15:30,830
a store at all.

471
00:15:31,010 --> 00:15:32,438
It came from a party.

472
00:15:32,618 --> 00:15:34,000
A Tupperware party.

473
00:15:34,180 --> 00:15:36,393
Tupperware's plastic containers,

474
00:15:36,393 --> 00:15:38,605
with their famous airtight seal,

475
00:15:38,605 --> 00:15:41,855
were sold almost entirely through home parties,

476
00:15:41,855 --> 00:15:44,414
hosted by women for their friends and

477
00:15:44,414 --> 00:15:45,105
neighbors.

478
00:15:45,455 --> 00:15:48,113
The hostess served coffee and cake.

479
00:15:48,293 --> 00:15:51,028
A saleswoman demonstrated the products,

480
00:15:51,028 --> 00:15:53,763
sometimes tossing a sealed bowl full of

481
00:15:53,763 --> 00:15:56,147
liquid across the room to prove it

482
00:15:56,147 --> 00:15:57,128
wouldn't leak.

483
00:15:57,308 --> 00:15:58,737
You burped the lid,

484
00:15:58,737 --> 00:16:00,240
to push out the air,

485
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:01,894
and everybody laughed.

486
00:16:02,074 --> 00:16:03,079
And at the end,

487
00:16:03,079 --> 00:16:04,754
everyone placed an order,

488
00:16:04,754 --> 00:16:06,764
and the hostess got a gift for

489
00:16:06,764 --> 00:16:07,903
having the party.

490
00:16:08,253 --> 00:16:09,297
For many women,

491
00:16:09,297 --> 00:16:12,014
those parties were about much more than

492
00:16:12,014 --> 00:16:12,989
plastic bowls.

493
00:16:13,169 --> 00:16:14,980
They were a social life,

494
00:16:14,980 --> 00:16:16,563
a reason to dress up,

495
00:16:16,563 --> 00:16:17,544
and for some,

496
00:16:17,544 --> 00:16:19,580
a real job and an income of

497
00:16:19,580 --> 00:16:21,843
their own, at a time when that

498
00:16:21,843 --> 00:16:23,351
wasn't easy to find.

499
00:16:23,531 --> 00:16:26,082
A lot of women built careers out

500
00:16:26,082 --> 00:16:28,872
of a living room full of neighbors.

501
00:16:29,221 --> 00:16:30,816
And if you look in your own

502
00:16:30,816 --> 00:16:32,411
kitchen cupboard right now,

503
00:16:32,411 --> 00:16:34,715
there's a decent chance you'll find one

504
00:16:34,715 --> 00:16:36,605
of those containers still there.

505
00:16:36,785 --> 00:16:37,812
Faded a little,

506
00:16:37,812 --> 00:16:39,316
maybe missing its lid.

507
00:16:39,496 --> 00:16:41,507
But still doing its job,

508
00:16:41,507 --> 00:16:43,014
sixty years later.

509
00:16:43,364 --> 00:16:44,463
Off the kitchen,

510
00:16:44,463 --> 00:16:45,424
in many homes,

511
00:16:45,424 --> 00:16:46,729
there was a pantry,

512
00:16:46,729 --> 00:16:48,926
or at least a deep cupboard with

513
00:16:48,926 --> 00:16:50,505
shelves to the ceiling.

514
00:16:50,685 --> 00:16:52,449
It was lined with cans.

515
00:16:52,629 --> 00:16:54,313
Soup, peaches, corn,

516
00:16:54,313 --> 00:16:57,764
evaporated milk, and coffee in tall metal

517
00:16:57,764 --> 00:17:00,373
cans that needed a key to open.

518
00:17:00,553 --> 00:17:02,847
There was a sack of potatoes on

519
00:17:02,847 --> 00:17:04,919
the floor, and an onion bin,

520
00:17:04,919 --> 00:17:07,213
and always more than one box of

521
00:17:07,213 --> 00:17:07,879
crackers.

522
00:17:08,229 --> 00:17:09,357
And in late summer,

523
00:17:09,357 --> 00:17:10,841
in a great many kitchens,

524
00:17:10,841 --> 00:17:11,910
there was canning.

525
00:17:12,090 --> 00:17:15,778
Your mother, maybe with your grandmother beside

526
00:17:15,778 --> 00:17:17,033
her, spent long,

527
00:17:17,033 --> 00:17:19,309
hot days putting up tomatoes,

528
00:17:19,309 --> 00:17:20,956
peaches, green beans,

529
00:17:20,956 --> 00:17:23,781
pickles and jam in glass Mason jars.

530
00:17:23,961 --> 00:17:25,470
The kitchen was steaming.

531
00:17:25,650 --> 00:17:27,762
The jars sat cooling on towels,

532
00:17:27,762 --> 00:17:29,261
and every few minutes,

533
00:17:29,261 --> 00:17:31,304
one would make a little pop as

534
00:17:31,304 --> 00:17:32,326
the lid sealed.

535
00:17:32,676 --> 00:17:35,121
Those jars went down to the basement

536
00:17:35,121 --> 00:17:37,565
shelves, and all winter long you ate

537
00:17:37,565 --> 00:17:38,040
summer.

538
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:41,199
A jar of peaches in January tasted

539
00:17:41,199 --> 00:17:42,075
like July.

540
00:17:42,255 --> 00:17:44,667
Today, people pay a lot of money

541
00:17:44,667 --> 00:17:47,758
for something called artisanal preserves.

542
00:17:47,938 --> 00:17:49,476
In nineteen sixty-two,

543
00:17:49,476 --> 00:17:52,062
your mother just called it putting up

544
00:17:52,062 --> 00:17:54,508
food, and she'd have laughed at the

545
00:17:54,508 --> 00:17:54,927
price.

546
00:17:55,277 --> 00:17:57,344
So what came out of that kitchen?

547
00:17:57,524 --> 00:17:59,272
In nineteen sixty-two,

548
00:17:59,272 --> 00:18:01,180
dinner was usually meat,

549
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:03,166
a starch and a vegetable.

550
00:18:03,346 --> 00:18:04,113
Pot roast.

551
00:18:04,293 --> 00:18:05,013
Meatloaf.

552
00:18:05,193 --> 00:18:06,237
Pork chops.

553
00:18:06,417 --> 00:18:07,799
Chicken on Sunday.

554
00:18:07,979 --> 00:18:09,837
Mashed potatoes with butter.

555
00:18:10,017 --> 00:18:11,871
Green beans, or peas,

556
00:18:11,871 --> 00:18:13,813
very often from a can.

557
00:18:13,993 --> 00:18:15,096
And on Friday,

558
00:18:15,096 --> 00:18:16,200
in many homes,

559
00:18:16,200 --> 00:18:16,594
fish.

560
00:18:16,943 --> 00:18:19,537
Canned and frozen food felt modern,

561
00:18:19,537 --> 00:18:21,019
and modern was good.

562
00:18:21,199 --> 00:18:24,252
Cream of mushroom soup went into everything.

563
00:18:24,432 --> 00:18:27,971
Casseroles topped with crushed potato chips or

564
00:18:27,971 --> 00:18:28,971
fried onions.

565
00:18:29,151 --> 00:18:31,741
Tuna noodle casserole on a weeknight.

566
00:18:31,921 --> 00:18:34,556
Green bean casserole at Thanksgiving.

567
00:18:34,736 --> 00:18:37,859
A whole generation of recipes started with

568
00:18:37,859 --> 00:18:40,239
the words, open one can of soup.

569
00:18:40,589 --> 00:18:41,914
When dinner was ready,

570
00:18:41,914 --> 00:18:43,840
your mother didn't text anybody.

571
00:18:44,020 --> 00:18:46,622
She opened the back door and called

572
00:18:46,622 --> 00:18:47,811
your name, once,

573
00:18:47,811 --> 00:18:50,263
twice, and if you didn't come the

574
00:18:50,263 --> 00:18:53,088
third time, she used your middle name.

575
00:18:53,268 --> 00:18:54,928
Then you knew you were in trouble.

576
00:18:55,108 --> 00:18:56,873
Some mothers rang a bell.

577
00:18:57,053 --> 00:18:58,782
Some fathers whistled.

578
00:18:58,962 --> 00:19:01,250
Every child knew their family's call.

579
00:19:01,599 --> 00:19:03,144
And then there was gelatin.

580
00:19:03,324 --> 00:19:05,170
Jell-O was everywhere.

581
00:19:05,350 --> 00:19:07,153
Not just for dessert,

582
00:19:07,153 --> 00:19:08,355
but in salads,

583
00:19:08,355 --> 00:19:11,532
molded into rings and stars in copper

584
00:19:11,532 --> 00:19:13,078
or aluminum molds,

585
00:19:13,078 --> 00:19:15,310
with fruit, or vegetables,

586
00:19:15,310 --> 00:19:18,144
or even meat suspended inside it.

587
00:19:18,324 --> 00:19:19,869
To your grandchildren,

588
00:19:19,869 --> 00:19:21,133
it sounds strange.

589
00:19:21,313 --> 00:19:23,046
In nineteen sixty-two,

590
00:19:23,046 --> 00:19:24,227
it was elegant.

591
00:19:24,577 --> 00:19:25,604
For busy nights,

592
00:19:25,604 --> 00:19:27,143
there was the TV dinner.

593
00:19:27,323 --> 00:19:29,941
Swanson had made them famous in the

594
00:19:29,941 --> 00:19:31,213
nineteen fifties.

595
00:19:31,393 --> 00:19:34,225
An aluminum tray with three compartments.

596
00:19:34,405 --> 00:19:37,505
Turkey, mashed potatoes and peas.

597
00:19:37,685 --> 00:19:39,439
You heated it in the oven for

598
00:19:39,439 --> 00:19:41,012
about twenty-five minutes,

599
00:19:41,012 --> 00:19:42,887
because there was no microwave.

600
00:19:43,067 --> 00:19:44,603
And you ate it on a folding

601
00:19:44,603 --> 00:19:46,252
metal TV tray in front of the

602
00:19:46,252 --> 00:19:46,480
set.

603
00:19:46,829 --> 00:19:49,430
The TV tray itself deserves a moment.

604
00:19:49,610 --> 00:19:51,304
It was a thin metal tray,

605
00:19:51,304 --> 00:19:54,284
painted with flowers or a woodgrain pattern,

606
00:19:54,284 --> 00:19:56,655
on folding legs that wobbled if you

607
00:19:56,655 --> 00:19:57,807
breathed on them.

608
00:19:57,987 --> 00:19:59,734
Families had sets of four,

609
00:19:59,734 --> 00:20:01,615
kept in a little rack in the

610
00:20:01,615 --> 00:20:02,085
corner.

611
00:20:02,265 --> 00:20:03,217
Bring them out,

612
00:20:03,217 --> 00:20:05,438
and suddenly dinner in front of the

613
00:20:05,438 --> 00:20:07,977
television felt like a special occasion.

614
00:20:08,327 --> 00:20:10,558
And that's another thing that kitchen didn't

615
00:20:10,558 --> 00:20:10,812
have.

616
00:20:10,992 --> 00:20:12,466
A microwave oven.

617
00:20:12,646 --> 00:20:15,549
The first countertop microwave for the home

618
00:20:15,549 --> 00:20:18,451
wouldn't arrive until nineteen sixty-seven.

619
00:20:18,631 --> 00:20:20,311
In nineteen sixty-two,

620
00:20:20,311 --> 00:20:23,059
everything was heated in the oven or

621
00:20:23,059 --> 00:20:24,051
on the stove,

622
00:20:24,051 --> 00:20:26,647
and leftovers took twenty minutes,

623
00:20:26,647 --> 00:20:27,258
not two.

624
00:20:27,607 --> 00:20:28,663
In the cupboard,

625
00:20:28,663 --> 00:20:30,643
there was a box of cereal with

626
00:20:30,643 --> 00:20:32,491
a toy at the bottom that the

627
00:20:32,491 --> 00:20:33,877
children fought over.

628
00:20:34,057 --> 00:20:35,871
And maybe a jar of Tang,

629
00:20:35,871 --> 00:20:38,669
the orange drink powder that came out

630
00:20:38,669 --> 00:20:40,408
in nineteen fifty-nine.

631
00:20:40,588 --> 00:20:41,940
A few years later,

632
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:44,421
it would fly into space with NASA

633
00:20:44,421 --> 00:20:47,502
astronauts, and sales would take off with

634
00:20:47,502 --> 00:20:47,728
it.

635
00:20:48,078 --> 00:20:49,381
Every school morning,

636
00:20:49,381 --> 00:20:51,864
the kitchen counter turned into a little

637
00:20:51,864 --> 00:20:52,733
assembly line.

638
00:20:52,913 --> 00:20:54,956
Bread laid out in rows.

639
00:20:55,136 --> 00:20:57,006
Peanut butter and jelly,

640
00:20:57,006 --> 00:20:58,875
or bologna with mustard.

641
00:20:59,055 --> 00:21:01,676
Each sandwich wrapped in waxed paper,

642
00:21:01,676 --> 00:21:03,234
folded at the corners.

643
00:21:03,414 --> 00:21:04,843
An apple, a cookie,

644
00:21:04,843 --> 00:21:05,895
and a thermos,

645
00:21:05,895 --> 00:21:08,677
all packed into a metal lunchbox with

646
00:21:08,677 --> 00:21:10,331
a picture on the side,

647
00:21:10,331 --> 00:21:12,586
or a brown paper bag with your

648
00:21:12,586 --> 00:21:14,014
name written on it.

649
00:21:14,364 --> 00:21:15,719
On Saturday mornings,

650
00:21:15,719 --> 00:21:18,236
the kitchen often filled with the smell

651
00:21:18,236 --> 00:21:18,881
of baking.

652
00:21:19,061 --> 00:21:20,598
Bread for the week,

653
00:21:20,598 --> 00:21:22,377
cinnamon rolls, a pie,

654
00:21:22,377 --> 00:21:24,076
or a cake for Sunday.

655
00:21:24,256 --> 00:21:26,695
A cake made from scratch was still

656
00:21:26,695 --> 00:21:29,135
common, though cake mixes in a box

657
00:21:29,135 --> 00:21:31,861
were becoming more popular every year.

658
00:21:32,041 --> 00:21:33,686
Some of them told you to add

659
00:21:33,686 --> 00:21:34,920
a fresh egg yourself,

660
00:21:34,920 --> 00:21:36,918
so the baker would feel like she'd

661
00:21:36,918 --> 00:21:37,799
really made it.

662
00:21:38,149 --> 00:21:39,891
And on special occasions,

663
00:21:39,891 --> 00:21:42,259
there was a homemade layer cake on

664
00:21:42,259 --> 00:21:44,419
a glass cake stand with a domed

665
00:21:44,419 --> 00:21:44,837
cover.

666
00:21:45,017 --> 00:21:46,436
It sat on the counter,

667
00:21:46,436 --> 00:21:47,274
in full view,

668
00:21:47,274 --> 00:21:49,982
where everybody could admire it and nobody

669
00:21:49,982 --> 00:21:52,240
was allowed to touch it until after

670
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:52,691
dinner.

671
00:21:52,871 --> 00:21:55,038
That cake stand might be the most

672
00:21:55,038 --> 00:21:57,467
tempting object in the history of the

673
00:21:57,467 --> 00:21:58,583
American kitchen.

674
00:21:58,933 --> 00:22:00,067
On the shelf,

675
00:22:00,067 --> 00:22:02,683
or on top of the refrigerator,

676
00:22:02,683 --> 00:22:05,386
sat a small metal or wooden box

677
00:22:05,386 --> 00:22:07,130
full of index cards.

678
00:22:07,310 --> 00:22:08,703
The recipe box.

679
00:22:08,883 --> 00:22:10,706
Every card was handwritten,

680
00:22:10,706 --> 00:22:12,664
many of them by women who are

681
00:22:12,664 --> 00:22:13,609
long gone now.

682
00:22:13,789 --> 00:22:15,530
Grandma's pie crust.

683
00:22:15,710 --> 00:22:17,684
Aunt Ruth's potato salad.

684
00:22:17,864 --> 00:22:19,524
A neighbor's coffee cake,

685
00:22:19,524 --> 00:22:21,184
with her name at the top.

686
00:22:21,534 --> 00:22:24,171
Those cards were splattered with butter and

687
00:22:24,171 --> 00:22:26,562
vanilla, and stained with coffee rings.

688
00:22:26,742 --> 00:22:28,831
Some had little notes in the margins.

689
00:22:29,011 --> 00:22:30,335
Use less sugar.

690
00:22:30,515 --> 00:22:32,314
Bake ten minutes longer.

691
00:22:32,494 --> 00:22:33,679
Harold's favorite.

692
00:22:33,859 --> 00:22:36,148
If you still have your mother's recipe

693
00:22:36,148 --> 00:22:37,955
box, you know that it's one of

694
00:22:37,955 --> 00:22:39,942
the most precious things you own.

695
00:22:40,292 --> 00:22:42,445
Next to the box stood a cookbook

696
00:22:42,445 --> 00:22:42,916
or two.

697
00:22:43,096 --> 00:22:45,708
The red and white checkered Better Homes

698
00:22:45,708 --> 00:22:47,079
and Gardens cookbook.

699
00:22:47,259 --> 00:22:49,403
The Betty Crocker cookbook,

700
00:22:49,403 --> 00:22:50,753
from a woman who,

701
00:22:50,753 --> 00:22:51,627
by the way,

702
00:22:51,627 --> 00:22:52,738
never existed.

703
00:22:52,918 --> 00:22:55,496
Betty Crocker was invented by a flour

704
00:22:55,496 --> 00:22:57,795
company in the nineteen twenties.

705
00:22:57,975 --> 00:23:00,866
But millions of women trusted her completely.

706
00:23:01,215 --> 00:23:03,751
And something new was happening in American

707
00:23:03,751 --> 00:23:04,222
cooking.

708
00:23:04,402 --> 00:23:06,518
In late nineteen sixty-one,

709
00:23:06,518 --> 00:23:08,947
a tall woman named Julia Child,

710
00:23:08,947 --> 00:23:10,514
with two co-authors,

711
00:23:10,514 --> 00:23:13,882
published a thick book called Mastering the

712
00:23:13,882 --> 00:23:15,606
Art of French Cooking.

713
00:23:15,786 --> 00:23:16,892
The next year,

714
00:23:16,892 --> 00:23:20,367
she appeared on public television in Boston,

715
00:23:20,367 --> 00:23:21,788
cooking an omelet.

716
00:23:21,968 --> 00:23:24,012
Soon she had her own show.

717
00:23:24,192 --> 00:23:27,756
Slowly, the American kitchen started to change.

718
00:23:28,106 --> 00:23:30,612
Here's something younger people find hard to

719
00:23:30,612 --> 00:23:31,067
believe.

720
00:23:31,247 --> 00:23:32,788
In nineteen sixty-two,

721
00:23:32,788 --> 00:23:35,031
a lot of what filled the kitchen

722
00:23:35,031 --> 00:23:37,273
came to the back door by itself.

723
00:23:37,453 --> 00:23:40,126
The milkman came several mornings a week,

724
00:23:40,126 --> 00:23:42,668
leaving bottles in a metal or insulated

725
00:23:42,668 --> 00:23:43,711
box on the step,

726
00:23:43,711 --> 00:23:45,015
and in cold weather,

727
00:23:45,015 --> 00:23:47,297
the cream would freeze and push the

728
00:23:47,297 --> 00:23:49,318
cap right up out of the bottle.

729
00:23:49,667 --> 00:23:51,826
The bread man came in some towns,

730
00:23:51,826 --> 00:23:52,872
and the egg man.

731
00:23:53,052 --> 00:23:55,291
The Fuller Brush man came with his

732
00:23:55,291 --> 00:23:57,793
case of brushes and cleaning products,

733
00:23:57,793 --> 00:23:59,966
and always had a little free gift

734
00:23:59,966 --> 00:24:01,678
for the lady of the house.

735
00:24:01,858 --> 00:24:03,578
And in many neighborhoods,

736
00:24:03,578 --> 00:24:06,026
a truck came around selling fruit and

737
00:24:06,026 --> 00:24:08,011
vegetables right off the back.

738
00:24:08,362 --> 00:24:10,150
The dishes, by the way,

739
00:24:10,150 --> 00:24:11,705
were washed by hand.

740
00:24:11,885 --> 00:24:14,281
Automatic dishwashers existed,

741
00:24:14,281 --> 00:24:17,317
but in nineteen sixty-two only a small

742
00:24:17,317 --> 00:24:19,873
share of American homes had one.

743
00:24:20,053 --> 00:24:21,261
So after dinner,

744
00:24:21,261 --> 00:24:23,905
somebody washed and somebody dried,

745
00:24:23,905 --> 00:24:26,473
standing side by side at the sink.

746
00:24:26,653 --> 00:24:29,295
Many of the best conversations of your

747
00:24:29,295 --> 00:24:31,450
childhood happened right there,

748
00:24:31,450 --> 00:24:32,702
over a dish towel.

749
00:24:33,052 --> 00:24:34,748
Then there were the holidays,

750
00:24:34,748 --> 00:24:36,912
when the kitchen became the center of

751
00:24:36,912 --> 00:24:37,672
the universe.

752
00:24:37,852 --> 00:24:40,891
Thanksgiving, when the turkey went into the

753
00:24:40,891 --> 00:24:42,092
oven before dawn,

754
00:24:42,092 --> 00:24:44,494
and the windows fogged up from all

755
00:24:44,494 --> 00:24:46,049
the pots on the stove.

756
00:24:46,229 --> 00:24:47,775
Every burner was in use,

757
00:24:47,775 --> 00:24:49,063
every bowl was full,

758
00:24:49,063 --> 00:24:51,253
and the women of the family worked

759
00:24:51,253 --> 00:24:53,958
shoulder to shoulder while the men watched

760
00:24:53,958 --> 00:24:55,633
football in the next room.

761
00:24:55,983 --> 00:24:57,634
And at those big dinners,

762
00:24:57,634 --> 00:25:00,011
the children sat at the kids' table.

763
00:25:00,191 --> 00:25:01,084
A card table,

764
00:25:01,084 --> 00:25:03,008
set up in the kitchen or the

765
00:25:03,008 --> 00:25:04,794
corner of the dining room,

766
00:25:04,794 --> 00:25:06,786
with a mismatched tablecloth.

767
00:25:06,966 --> 00:25:09,075
You spent years trying to graduate to

768
00:25:09,075 --> 00:25:10,158
the grown-up table.

769
00:25:10,338 --> 00:25:11,765
And then, one year,

770
00:25:11,765 --> 00:25:12,966
you finally did,

771
00:25:12,966 --> 00:25:15,444
and somehow it was never quite as

772
00:25:15,444 --> 00:25:16,120
much fun.

773
00:25:16,471 --> 00:25:17,413
Above the sink,

774
00:25:17,413 --> 00:25:18,607
there was a window.

775
00:25:18,787 --> 00:25:20,433
And on the windowsill,

776
00:25:20,433 --> 00:25:21,556
a little radio,

777
00:25:21,556 --> 00:25:22,828
a plant in a pot,

778
00:25:22,828 --> 00:25:24,998
and maybe a ceramic figurine.

779
00:25:25,178 --> 00:25:27,450
Your mother looked out of that window

780
00:25:27,450 --> 00:25:28,863
a thousand times a day,

781
00:25:28,863 --> 00:25:30,705
watching you play in the yard,

782
00:25:30,705 --> 00:25:32,978
waiting for your father's car to turn

783
00:25:32,978 --> 00:25:34,083
into the driveway.

784
00:25:34,433 --> 00:25:36,954
That little radio kept her company all

785
00:25:36,954 --> 00:25:37,219
day.

786
00:25:37,399 --> 00:25:38,688
The morning news.

787
00:25:38,868 --> 00:25:41,457
The farm report in rural towns.

788
00:25:41,637 --> 00:25:44,400
Music, soap operas that had moved over

789
00:25:44,400 --> 00:25:47,381
from radio to television but still played

790
00:25:47,381 --> 00:25:48,472
in some places,

791
00:25:48,472 --> 00:25:50,871
and a voice like Paul Harvey's at

792
00:25:50,871 --> 00:25:53,635
lunchtime, telling her the rest of the

793
00:25:53,635 --> 00:25:54,071
story.

794
00:25:54,251 --> 00:25:56,457
The kitchen was never truly quiet.

795
00:25:56,807 --> 00:25:59,361
Under the sink sat the cleaning supplies.

796
00:25:59,541 --> 00:26:01,584
A can of scouring powder.

797
00:26:01,764 --> 00:26:03,947
A box of soap flakes.

798
00:26:04,127 --> 00:26:05,973
Steel wool pads.

799
00:26:06,153 --> 00:26:07,627
A bottle of bleach.

800
00:26:07,807 --> 00:26:10,255
And the brown paper grocery bags,

801
00:26:10,255 --> 00:26:11,516
folded and saved,

802
00:26:11,516 --> 00:26:14,483
because they were useful for everything.

803
00:26:14,663 --> 00:26:16,497
Covering school books.

804
00:26:16,677 --> 00:26:18,442
Lining the garbage can.

805
00:26:18,622 --> 00:26:19,864
Draining the bacon.

806
00:26:20,215 --> 00:26:22,218
And somewhere in that kitchen,

807
00:26:22,218 --> 00:26:23,955
in a drawer or a cupboard,

808
00:26:23,955 --> 00:26:25,892
were the trading stamp books.

809
00:26:26,072 --> 00:26:28,665
Your mother collected the stamps at the

810
00:26:28,665 --> 00:26:30,926
grocery store and the gas station,

811
00:26:30,926 --> 00:26:32,522
and on rainy afternoons,

812
00:26:32,522 --> 00:26:34,849
you licked them and pasted them in.

813
00:26:35,029 --> 00:26:37,094
When enough books were full,

814
00:26:37,094 --> 00:26:39,822
you went to the redemption center and

815
00:26:39,822 --> 00:26:41,519
traded them for a lamp,

816
00:26:41,519 --> 00:26:42,994
or a set of glasses,

817
00:26:42,994 --> 00:26:44,247
or a new toaster.

818
00:26:44,598 --> 00:26:45,471
On the wall,

819
00:26:45,471 --> 00:26:46,782
above the doorway,

820
00:26:46,782 --> 00:26:48,603
ticked the kitchen clock.

821
00:26:48,783 --> 00:26:50,269
Maybe a sunburst.

822
00:26:50,449 --> 00:26:52,890
Maybe a cat whose tail swung back

823
00:26:52,890 --> 00:26:55,478
and forth and whose eyes moved with

824
00:26:55,478 --> 00:26:56,440
every second.

825
00:26:56,620 --> 00:26:58,326
That clock ran the house.

826
00:26:58,506 --> 00:26:59,676
When it said six,

827
00:26:59,676 --> 00:27:01,328
dinner was on the table.

828
00:27:01,508 --> 00:27:02,743
When it said eight,

829
00:27:02,743 --> 00:27:04,108
children went to bed.

830
00:27:04,459 --> 00:27:05,527
And there was,

831
00:27:05,527 --> 00:27:08,197
almost always, a woman in an apron.

832
00:27:08,377 --> 00:27:11,372
The kitchen of nineteen sixty-two was built

833
00:27:11,372 --> 00:27:12,139
around her.

834
00:27:12,319 --> 00:27:14,277
She cooked three meals a day,

835
00:27:14,277 --> 00:27:15,965
planned the week's menus,

836
00:27:15,965 --> 00:27:17,924
stretched the grocery budget,

837
00:27:17,924 --> 00:27:19,679
and made it all look easy.

838
00:27:19,859 --> 00:27:21,055
It wasn't easy.

839
00:27:21,235 --> 00:27:22,031
Many of you,

840
00:27:22,031 --> 00:27:23,158
looking back now,

841
00:27:23,158 --> 00:27:25,677
understand that better than you did at

842
00:27:25,677 --> 00:27:26,274
the time.

843
00:27:26,623 --> 00:27:28,049
Later in the sixties,

844
00:27:28,049 --> 00:27:29,747
the kitchen would change.

845
00:27:29,927 --> 00:27:31,355
Avocado green.

846
00:27:31,535 --> 00:27:33,114
Bigger refrigerators.

847
00:27:33,294 --> 00:27:35,260
Microwaves, and dishwashers,

848
00:27:35,260 --> 00:27:37,717
and convenience foods by the aisle.

849
00:27:37,897 --> 00:27:39,402
And within a generation,

850
00:27:39,402 --> 00:27:41,848
many women would leave that kitchen for

851
00:27:41,848 --> 00:27:42,977
jobs of their own,

852
00:27:42,977 --> 00:27:45,046
and the whole rhythm of the house

853
00:27:45,046 --> 00:27:46,488
would change with them.

854
00:27:46,839 --> 00:27:48,163
But for a few years,

855
00:27:48,163 --> 00:27:49,886
around nineteen sixty-two,

856
00:27:49,886 --> 00:27:52,734
the American kitchen was exactly what we've

857
00:27:52,734 --> 00:27:54,257
walked through tonight.

858
00:27:54,437 --> 00:27:55,494
The percolator.

859
00:27:55,674 --> 00:27:57,915
The wall phone with its tangled cord.

860
00:27:58,095 --> 00:28:00,927
The Pyrex bowls and the canister set.

861
00:28:01,107 --> 00:28:02,663
The church key in the drawer.

862
00:28:02,843 --> 00:28:04,306
The recipe box.

863
00:28:04,486 --> 00:28:06,367
And a mother in an apron,

864
00:28:06,367 --> 00:28:07,346
at the stove,

865
00:28:07,346 --> 00:28:08,700
with the radio on.

866
00:28:09,051 --> 00:28:10,815
That kitchen is gone now.

867
00:28:10,995 --> 00:28:12,616
But if you grew up in it,

868
00:28:12,616 --> 00:28:14,043
you can still walk in,

869
00:28:14,043 --> 00:28:15,210
any time you like.

870
00:28:15,390 --> 00:28:16,794
Just close your eyes.

871
00:28:16,974 --> 00:28:18,704
You'll hear the percolator.

872
00:28:18,884 --> 00:28:20,394
You'll smell the coffee.

873
00:28:20,574 --> 00:28:23,033
And somebody will be calling your name,

874
00:28:23,033 --> 00:28:25,241
telling you dinner is almost ready.

875
00:28:25,591 --> 00:28:27,413
So tell me in the comments.

876
00:28:27,593 --> 00:28:29,946
What did your mother's kitchen have that

877
00:28:29,946 --> 00:28:31,181
nobody remembers now?

878
00:28:31,361 --> 00:28:32,638
What color was it?

879
00:28:32,818 --> 00:28:34,321
And what was the one dish she

880
00:28:34,321 --> 00:28:36,292
made that you'd give anything to taste

881
00:28:36,292 --> 00:28:36,603
again?

882
00:28:36,783 --> 00:28:38,105
I read every comment,

883
00:28:38,105 --> 00:28:40,119
and the best memories may end up

884
00:28:40,119 --> 00:28:41,253
in a future video.

885
00:28:41,603 --> 00:28:43,466
And if you'd like to keep going

886
00:28:43,466 --> 00:28:45,691
back, our next journey is waiting for

887
00:28:45,691 --> 00:28:46,653
you, right here.

888
00:28:46,833 --> 00:28:48,615
Thank you for spending this evening with

889
00:28:48,615 --> 00:28:48,749
me.

890
00:28:48,929 --> 00:28:50,121
Until next time,

891
00:28:50,121 --> 00:28:51,761
take care of yourself.
